\pard\qc\b\fs36 TaskFlow Microservice Application\par
\fs30 Kubernetes Deployment Practical\par
\b0\fs22\par
\pard\ql
\b Student:\b0 ______________________________\par
\b Date:\b0 ______________________________\par
\par
\b 1. Aim\b0\par
Deploy the TaskFlow microservice application on Kubernetes after Docker containerization. Components: React frontend, user-service, task-service, stats-service, and MongoDB.\par
\par
\b 2. Ports\b0\par
user-service: 4001\par
task-service: 4002\par
stats-service: 4003\par
MongoDB: 27017\par
frontend container: 80\par
\par
\b 3. Kubernetes Files\b0\par
The following files are inside the project folder \f1 k8s\f0 :\par
\f1 00-namespace.yaml\par
01-secret.yaml\par
02-configmap.yaml\par
03-mongodb.yaml\par
04-user-service.yaml\par
05-task-service.yaml\par
06-stats-service.yaml\par
07-frontend.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 4. Docker Compose Test\b0\par
Open PowerShell in the project root:\par
\f1 cd "C:\\Users\\ROHAN PRAJAPATI\\Desktop\\sem7\\cloud native and devops\\lab\\microservice_app\\taskflow"\par
docker compose up --build\par
docker compose ps\par
\f0\par
This confirms that the application works before Kubernetes deployment.\par
\par
\b 5. Kubernetes Check\b0\par
\f1 docker --version\par
kubectl version --client\par
kubectl config current-context\par
kubectl get nodes\par
\f0\par
Expected context: \f1 docker-desktop\f0 . Expected node status: \f1 Ready\f0 .\par
\par
\b 6. Namespace\b0\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/00-namespace.yaml\par
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=taskflow\par
\f0\par
\b 7. Configuration\b0\par
Apply credentials and application configuration:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/01-secret.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/02-configmap.yaml\par
\f0\par
Kubernetes internal names:\par
\f1 mongodb:27017\par
http://user-service:4001\par
http://task-service:4002\par
http://stats-service:4003\par
\f0\par
\b 8. Build Images\b0\par
\f1 docker build -t taskflow-user-service:latest ./services/user-service\par
docker build -t taskflow-task-service:latest ./services/task-service\par
docker build -t taskflow-stats-service:latest ./services/stats-service\par
docker build -t taskflow-frontend:latest ./frontend\par
docker images\par
\f0\par
\b 9. Frontend Routing\b0\par
The frontend uses relative paths such as \f1 /api/users\f0 . Nginx proxies these paths to the Kubernetes Services. This avoids using \f1 localhost\f0 or browser-inaccessible Kubernetes DNS names.\par
\par
Rebuild the frontend after changing \f1 frontend/src/api.js\f0 and \f1 frontend/nginx.conf\f0 :\par
\f1 cd frontend\par
npm run build\par
cd ..\par
\f0\par
\b 10. Validate Manifests\b0\par
\f1 kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f k8s/\par
\f0\par
Expected result: all resources report \f1 created (dry run)\f0 .\par
\par
\b 11. Deploy MongoDB\b0\par
MongoDB uses a PersistentVolumeClaim for storage:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/03-mongodb.yaml\par
kubectl get pods -n taskflow\par
kubectl get services -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
\b 12. Deploy Backend\b0\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/04-user-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/05-task-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/06-stats-service.yaml\par
\f0\par
\b 13. Deploy Frontend\b0\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/07-frontend.yaml\par
\f0\par
The frontend Service uses NodePort 30080.\par
\par
\b 14. Complete Deployment\b0\par
All manifests can also be applied together:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/\par
kubectl wait --for=condition=available deployment --all -n taskflow --timeout=180s\par
\f0\par
\b 15. Status Check\b0\par
\f1 kubectl get all -n taskflow\par
kubectl get pods -n taskflow -o wide\par
kubectl get services -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Expected pod status: \f1 1/1 Running\f0 . Expected frontend Service: \f1 80:30080/TCP\f0 .\par
\par
\b 16. Health Test\b0\par
\f1 kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/user-service -- wget -qO- http://localhost:4001/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/task-service -- wget -qO- http://localhost:4002/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/stats-service -- wget -qO- http://localhost:4003/health\par
\f0\par
Expected response contains \f1 {"status":"ok"}\f0 .\par
\par
\b 17. Service DNS Test\b0\par
\f1 kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://user-service:4001/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://task-service:4002/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://stats-service:4003/health\par
\f0\par
\b 18. API Proxy Test\b0\par
\f1 kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1/api/users\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1/api/tasks\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1/api/stats/overview\par
\f0\par
Expected initial result: empty users and tasks lists, and zero statistics.\par
\par
\b 19. Open Application\b0\par
Use port-forwarding because Docker Desktop did not expose NodePort directly on localhost:\par
\f1 kubectl port-forward -n taskflow service/frontend 8080:80\par
\f0\par
Open in a browser: \f1 http://localhost:8080\f0 . Stop forwarding with \f1 Ctrl+C\f0 .\par
\par
\b 20. Logs\b0\par
\f1 kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/user-service\par
kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/task-service\par
kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/stats-service\par
kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/frontend\par
kubectl logs -n taskflow deployment/mongodb\par
kubectl describe pod -n taskflow <pod-name>\par
kubectl get events -n taskflow --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\par
\f0\par
\b 21. Scaling\b0\par
\f1 kubectl scale deployment user-service --replicas=3 -n taskflow\par
kubectl scale deployment task-service --replicas=3 -n taskflow\par
kubectl scale deployment stats-service --replicas=2 -n taskflow\par
kubectl get pods -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
\b 22. Update and Rollback\b0\par
\f1 docker build -t taskflow-task-service:1.1 ./services/task-service\par
kubectl set image deployment/task-service task-service=taskflow-task-service:1.1 -n taskflow\par
kubectl rollout status deployment/task-service -n taskflow\par
kubectl rollout undo deployment/task-service -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
\b 23. Service Discovery\b0\par
The backend Services already use the ClusterIP type. Kubernetes provides DNS names for these Services:\par
\f1 user-service.taskflow.svc.cluster.local\par
task-service.taskflow.svc.cluster.local\par
stats-service.taskflow.svc.cluster.local\par
mongodb.taskflow.svc.cluster.local\par
\f0\par
Short names can be used from Pods in the same namespace:\par
\f1 user-service\par
task-service\par
stats-service\par
mongodb\par
\f0\par
Check the Services and their endpoints:\par
\f1 kubectl get services -n taskflow\par
kubectl get endpoints -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Test Kubernetes DNS resolution:\par
\f1 kubectl run dns-test --rm -i --restart=Never --image=busybox:1.36 -- nslookup user-service.taskflow.svc.cluster.local\par
\f0\par
Test communication through Service DNS names:\par
\f1 kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://user-service:4001/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://task-service:4002/health\par
kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://stats-service:4003/health\par
\f0\par
\b 24. Load Balancing\b0\par
The backend Deployments were configured with three replicas. The changes were made in:\par
\f1 k8s/04-user-service.yaml\par
k8s/05-task-service.yaml\par
k8s/06-stats-service.yaml\par
\f0\par
The Deployment setting is:\par
\f1 replicas: 3\par
\f0\par
Apply the updated Deployments:\par
\f1 kubectl apply -f k8s/04-user-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/05-task-service.yaml\par
kubectl apply -f k8s/06-stats-service.yaml\par
kubectl wait --for=condition=available deployment/user-service deployment/task-service deployment/stats-service -n taskflow --timeout=180s\par
\f0\par
Verify the three replicas and the Service endpoints:\par
\f1 kubectl get pods -n taskflow -l "app in (user-service,task-service,stats-service)" -o wide\par
kubectl get endpoints user-service task-service stats-service -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Each Service displayed three Pod IP addresses. Kubernetes load-balances requests sent to the stable ClusterIP across the ready Pods selected by the Service labels.\par
\par
Generate repeated requests through the user-service Service:\par
\f1 1..6 | ForEach-Object { kubectl exec -n taskflow deployment/frontend -- wget -qO- http://user-service:4001/health }\par
\f0\par
\b 25. Self-Healing Test\b0\par
Delete one user-service Pod:\par
\f1 kubectl delete pod <user-service-pod-name> -n taskflow --wait=false\par
kubectl wait --for=condition=available deployment/user-service -n taskflow --timeout=120s\par
kubectl get pods -n taskflow -l app=user-service -o wide\par
kubectl get endpoints user-service -n taskflow\par
\f0\par
Kubernetes automatically created a replacement Pod, and the Service continued to expose three ready endpoints.\par
\par
\b 26. Discovery and Load Balancing Result\b0\par
Service Discovery was verified using Kubernetes DNS. Load balancing was verified using three replicas per backend and three endpoint addresses per Service. Self-healing was verified by deleting one Pod and observing its automatic replacement.\par
\par
\b 27. Cleanup\b0\par
\f1 kubectl delete -f k8s/\par
kubectl delete namespace taskflow\par
\f0\par
\b 28. Result\b0\par
The TaskFlow application was successfully containerized and deployed to Docker Desktop Kubernetes. MongoDB, all backend services, and the frontend reached the Running state. Health checks, Kubernetes DNS checks, API proxy checks, and frontend access through \f1 http://localhost:8080\f0 were successful.\par
\par
\b 29. Conclusion\b0\par
This practical demonstrated Docker Compose, Docker images, Kubernetes Namespace, Secret, ConfigMap, PersistentVolumeClaim, Deployments, Services, health probes, service discovery, scaling, rollout, rollback, logging, and application verification.\par
